{"qid":"Q811","names":["Republic of Nicaragua"],"title":"Republic of Nicaragua","endonym":{"native":"República de Nicaragua","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Nicaragua declared independence from Spain as part of the Central American Federation in 1821 and became a fully independent republic when that federation dissolved in 1838, though the country endured chronic civil war between Liberal and Conservative factions through the nineteenth century, including the filibuster occupation of William Walker (1855–57). The Somoza family dictatorship dominated from 1936 until the Sandinista revolution of July 1979, which installed a left-wing junta that then faced a US-backed Contra insurgency throughout the 1980s. Sandinista leader Daniel Ortega lost the 1990 elections to Violeta Chamorro, beginning a decade of democratic transition, but Ortega returned to the presidency in 2007 and progressively dismantled independent institutions, consolidating authoritarian rule by the 2020s. Nicaragua is Central America's largest country by area.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Modern borders are well-defined; a long-running ICJ boundary dispute with Colombia over Caribbean maritime zones was resolved in 2012 but implementation remains contested.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Nicaragua","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Nicaragua"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Nicaragua","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicaragua"}],"britannica":"place/Nicaragua","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Nicaragua","rulers":[{"qid":"Q887755","name":"Fruto Chamorro","start":1854,"end":1855,"image":"01 Fruto Chamorro Pérez 1854-1855.png","imageU